
[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mClassification: bounded[0m — this is a single-file change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, so I'll present a short design in chat rather
  than write a spec. If it turns out you want an item model and list rendering behind it, that's new structure and I'll
  say so and step up to the heavier path.

  Current state: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is an empty shell — an [38;5;153m<h1>Tasks</h1>[39m and an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. No JavaScript, no CSS, no build
  tooling. So whatever I add establishes the first pattern on this page.

  One thing I need from you before designing.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the checkbox is one hard-coded control in the HTML, or a thing the page creates per task item. That
  decides whether this page gets JavaScript at all.

  - [1mStatic markup only[0m — one [38;5;153m<label><input type="checkbox"> …</label>[39m written directly into [38;5;153m<main>[39m. Zero JS, the browser
    handles the on/off state. Cheapest, but every additional item is another hand-edited block, and nothing else can
    read "is this done."
  - [1mA JS render function[0m — a small [38;5;153mcreateCheckbox(label)[39m (or equivalent) that builds the element, plus an array of items
    to render. Costs a [38;5;153m<script>[39m block and an item model, but adding items is data, not markup. This is the one that's
    expensive to retrofit later if you start static and grow.

  Persistence across reload is a separate question I'll ask after this one; it's cheap to add either way.

  [1mMy recommendation: static markup only.[0m You said basic and nothing fancy, and a native [38;5;153m<input type="checkbox">[39m already
  gives you on/off state with no code. Going to JS is easy later; the reverse is the throwaway.
